.TH OOBE-GXWI 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
oobe-gxwi \- Peios first-boot setup, in a browser, as GXWI's overlay
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oobe-gxwi
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR ADDRESS : PORT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
First-boot setup in a browser, on a machine that has been installed and not
set up. Where it is installed,
.BR oobed (8)
makes it GXWI's overlay while setup is pending: it makes a passwordless
account to run it as, admits that account to its socket, and sets
.I OverlayUsername
and
.I OverlaySession
in
.IR Machine\eSoftware\eGXWI .
Everyone who opens the machine's address in a browser is then sent here, with
no logon. Without it, setup is offered on the machine's console only.
.PP
It does nothing to the machine itself.
.B oobed
makes the first account and names the machine, as SYSTEM, and asks its
questions over a socket; this program holds that conversation and serves a
page that draws it: the welcome, the network and an address set by hand, the
account, the machine's name, and finishing. A password typed is the browser's
own until Next, and is never sent to anyone else looking; opened by an address
that is not the loopback, the page says the password will cross the network
unencrypted.
.PP
.BR oobed 's
last act is to take the overlay away, which ends this program and gives the
address back to GXWI's sign-in page. The page follows it there.
.PP
It is the same program as
.BR installer-gxwi (1),
built twice, because the two are one page: a restart from the installer lands
here.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
.BR oobed 's
socket.
The default is
.IR /run/oobed.sock .
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDRESS" : PORT
Serve here instead of on a socket handed over, for working on the page where
there is no GXWI.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of these.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR installer-gxwi (1),
.BR oobed (8),
.BR gxwid (1)
